---
import StarlightPage from '@astrojs/starlight/components/StarlightPage.astro';
---
<StarlightPage
  frontmatter={{
    title: 'Testing prompts and skills',
    description: 'How Maelstrom prompt evaluation controls fixtures, comparisons, and evidence.',
  }}
>
  <article class="evaluation-guide">
    <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/agents/prompts/">Agent prompts</a> / Testing prompts and skills
    </nav>
    <header>
      <p class="eyebrow">AGENT EVALUATION</p>
      <h1>Testing prompts and skills</h1>
      <p>
        A strong prompt or skill should help on new work, not only reproduce a known
        answer. The evaluation harness separates task evidence from the guidance
        being tested and keeps its limits visible.
      </p>
    </header>
    <section>
      <h2>Keep the task fixed</h2>
      <p>
        Each case starts from the same fresh, unsolved task fixture. Known-good and
        deliberately broken calibration projects test the grader; they are not
        agent answers. Every run has a task. A canonical prompt is optional, and
        skills may be available without being discovered or used.
      </p>
      <p>The four conceptual conditions are:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Task only</li>
        <li>Task plus canonical prompt</li>
        <li>Task plus available skills</li>
        <li>Task plus canonical prompt and available skills</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Supplying a skill tests following it, not discovering it. To compare
        conditions, hold fixture, model and CLI settings, permissions, budget, and
        grader constant. Repeat independent runs before comparing reliability,
        elapsed time, tokens, or cost—and report only measures actually collected.
        No condition guarantees better results.
      </p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h2>Preserve failures and distinguish evidence</h2>
      <p>
        Compare a baseline before a prompt or skill change with the new version on
        fresh fixture copies. Keep the original result and retain concrete bugs as
        regression cases. Do not weaken checks or tune only the known cases to
        improve a result. Version and schema guards verify contracts, not behavior.
      </p>
      <p>
        Objective checks can verify evidence structure, source quotations, and
        filesystem changes. They do not replace independent semantic review. The
        local simulated connector is not a live Engine or provider. See the
        <a href="/agents/prompts/">canonical prompt library</a>,
        <a href="/agents/skills/">portable skills</a>, and the local source
        guide at <code>apps/docs/prompts/evaluation/README.md</code> for their
        separate roles.
      </p>
    </section>
    <aside>
      The current harness includes five canonical-prompt cases and objective
      grading. Automatic prompt/skill A/B runs, repeated old/new summaries, and
      release thresholds are not implemented. Skill suppression is a deliberate
      baseline condition, not an automatic CLI switch.
    </aside>
  </article>
</StarlightPage>
